AB  - The constructivist method of teaching has been shown to have a major influence on the learning outcomes of students. In order to improve classroom instruction, it is essential to evaluate students' levels of involvement and attentiveness while they are being taught. Electroencephalography (EEG) can now be used to assess students' attentiveness in real time thanks to recent neuro-engineering breakthroughs and mobile brainwave sensors' availability. The purpose of this study is to provide an overview of EEG-based techniques with the intention of predicting constructivism and engagement among students of varying levels. The purpose of this study is to evaluate the efficacy of these approaches and to determine the extent to which they have the potential to enhance the design of future learning systems. This survey analyzes whether or not approaches based on EEG may be used to determine the level of constructivism and involvement displayed by pupils. The findings provide helpful insights that can be used to develop tailored teaching strategies and improve education's overall efficiency. However, some obstacles need to be overcome in order for EEG-based predictions to be accurate. Some of these obstacles include data quality and variability, individual differences, interpretability, generalization, and ethical considerations.
ER  - 

