AB  - Depression, as a common mental disorder, has increasingly attracted societal attention. The diagnosis of depression often requires a lengthy process. This paper proposes a method for preventing depression by providing early warning through the detection of depressive moods in the morning. The experiment collected electrocardiogram and three-dimensional acceleration signals from 82 individuals, analyzing and processing data from 7:55 to 8:00 in the morning and extracting 23 features. Six machine learning models were trained using leave-one-out cross-validation, and the voting decision model exhibited the best performance, with an accuracy of 73%, a recall rate of 85.53%, and an F1 score of 78.31%. The method in this study is operationally simple, utilizes wearable devices, and requires minimal time, bringing strong practical value. A drawback of the experiment is that the study population consisted of university students aged 18–25, which may not be representative of all groups. Therefore, future research should aim to broaden the study population.
